---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro'
import DocsShell, { GUIDE } from '../../../components/DocsShell.astro'
import Code from '../../../components/Code.astro'

const page = GUIDE.find((p) => p.slug === 'cardinality')!

const DECLARE = `@NodeEntity()
export class Person {
  @Uuid({ primary: true })
  id: string

  @OneToOne({
    type: 'LIVES_AT',
    target: () => Address,
    direction: 'out',
    cardinality: 'one',           // at most one edge — a second relate() throws
    enforce: 'currentOccupantId', // @Unique property on Address
    claimConflict: 'throw',       // 'refuse' (default) | 'detach' | 'throw'
  })
  address: Address | undefined
}

@NodeEntity()
export class Address {
  @Uuid({ primary: true })
  id: string

  // the claim record: which person claims this address.
  // @Unique is what turns the declaration into a database guarantee
  @String({ unique: true })
  currentOccupantId: string | undefined
}`

const SWAP = `// move out and in — both swap the edge AND move the claim in one
// statement (the old target's claim is removed, the new one is set)
await neode.syncEdges(Person, person, 'address', [newAddress])
await neode.replaceRelationship(person, 'address', [newAddress])

// release without a replacement
await neode.syncEdges(Person, person, 'address', [])`

const POLICIES = `// the target is already claimed by a different source:
await neode.relate(Person, personB, claimedAddress, 'address')

// claimConflict: 'refuse' (default)
//   -> matches zero rows, resolves undefined — nothing is stolen
// claimConflict: 'detach'
//   -> the other claimant's edges are deleted in the same statement
//      and the claim moves to personB
// claimConflict: 'throw'
//   -> NeodeError naming the current claimant and the fix`

const NESTED = `// nested creates claim at every level, four deep or more:
await neode.create(Person, {
  name: 'P',
  address: {
    name: 'home',
    building: {
      name: '221B',
      street: { name: 'Baker Street' },
    },
  },
})

// person claims the address, the address claims the building,
// the building claims the street — each claim referencing the SAME
// generated primary keys the nodes were written with`

const ENFORCED_WHERE = [
  { what: 'One target per source (forward side)', where: 'Application guard', how: 'cardinality one / zeroOrOne — the count-guard before every write' },
  { what: 'One source per target (inverse side)', where: 'Database', how: 'the @Unique property on the target backs the claim — raw Cypher included' },
  { what: 'Claim moves', where: 'neode, one statement', how: 'replaceRelationship() / syncEdges() detach + SET atomically' },
  { what: 'Claims released', where: 'neode', how: 'delete() / deleteAll() remove the property before the edges go' },
  { what: 'Concurrent claims on one target', where: 'Database', how: 'the SET serialises on the node lock; the loser gets ConstraintViolationError' },
]

const toc = [
  { href: '#declare', label: 'Declaring cardinality' },
  { href: '#claims', label: 'Claims: enforce' },
  { href: '#policies', label: 'claimConflict' },
  { href: '#enforced-where', label: 'What is enforced where' },
  { href: '#deletes', label: 'Deletes and swaps' },
  { href: '#nested', label: 'Nested creates' },
  { href: '#concurrency', label: 'Concurrency' },
]
---

<Base title="neode — Cardinality & claims">
  <DocsShell current="cardinality" toc={toc} prev={{ href: 'relationships', label: 'Relationships' }} next={{ href: 'traversals', label: 'Traversals' }}>
    <h1 style="margin:0 0 14px;font-size:clamp(32px,4vw,44px);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.02em">{page.title}</h1>
    <p class="txt" style="font-size:18px">
      Neo4j has no edge-count constraints. neode enforces both sides of a relationship anyway: the
      <strong>forward side</strong> (one target per source) as a write-time guard, and the
      <strong>inverse side</strong> (one source per target) as a <em>database-level</em> guarantee backed by a
      uniqueness constraint.
    </p>

    <h2 class="sec" id="declare">Declaring cardinality</h2>
    <p class="txt">
      <code>cardinality</code> takes <code>'one'</code>, <code>'zeroOrOne'</code> or <code>'many'</code>.
      <code>@OneToOne</code> defaults to <code>'one'</code>, <code>@OneToMany</code> to <code>'many'</code> — override it
      either way. A write that would leave a restricted relationship with more than one edge throws
      <code>CardinalityViolationError</code> before anything is written.
    </p>
    <Code code={DECLARE} />
    <p class="txt">
      Swap an edge with <code>syncEdges()</code> or <code>replaceRelationship()</code> — both detach first, so a
      <code>'one'</code> relationship never transiently holds two edges.
    </p>

    <h2 class="sec" id="claims">Claims: <code>enforce</code></h2>
    <p class="txt">
      The forward guard cannot help when two <em>different</em> sources relate to the same target — two people can each
      call the same address home. <code>enforce</code> closes that side: it names a <code>@Unique</code> property on the
      target model, and every write path keeps it pointed at the source node's primary-key value. The target's
      uniqueness constraint — installed by <code>installSchema()</code> — then rejects a second claim <em>at the
      database level</em>, raw Cypher included, and existing data is validated when the constraint is created.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      Requirements, validated the first time the relationship is written: the named property must exist on the target and
      be <code>@Unique</code>, cardinality must be <code>'one'</code> or <code>'zeroOrOne'</code>, and the source model
      needs a primary key carrying a value. <code>enforce</code> on <code>'many'</code> is rejected.
    </p>

    <h2 class="sec" id="policies"><code>claimConflict</code></h2>
    <p class="txt">
      When <code>relate()</code> targets a node whose claim names a different source, the policy decides what happens:
    </p>
    <Code code={POLICIES} />
    <dl style="margin:0 0 8px">
      <div class="dl"><dt>'refuse' (default)</dt><dd>The statement matches zero rows and relate() resolves <code>undefined</code> — the claim is never stolen</dd></div>
      <div class="dl"><dt>'detach'</dt><dd>The other claimant's edges are deleted in the same statement and the claim moves — a take-over</dd></div>
      <div class="dl"><dt>'throw'</dt><dd>A cheap probe distinguishes the conflict from a missing target and fails loudly, naming the current claimant</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="sec" id="enforced-where">What is enforced where</h2>
    <dl style="margin:0 0 8px">
      {ENFORCED_WHERE.map((row) => (
        <div class="dl"><dt>{row.what}</dt><dd><strong>{row.where}</strong> — {row.how}</dd></div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h2 class="sec" id="deletes">Deletes, swaps and nested creates</h2>
    <p class="txt">
      <code>delete()</code> and <code>deleteAll()</code> release the claims the deleted nodes hold before the edges go —
      at every level of a cascade, so nothing below the root goes stale. Soft deletes keep the node and its claims.
      Nested creates claim at every level, and generated <code>@Uuid</code> primaries are resolved once so the claim
      always references the value the node was actually written with.
    </p>
    <Code code={NESTED} />

    <h2 class="sec" id="concurrency">Concurrency</h2>
    <p class="txt">
      The inverse side is enforced by the database: two writers claiming the same target serialise on the node lock and
      the loser gets <code>ConstraintViolationError</code>. The forward side is enforced by neode: two writers
      <em>first-claiming</em> different targets concurrently can both succeed — Neo4j has no edge-count constraint — so
      serialise writers, or stack <code>@Version</code> for optimistic locking, when that must never happen.
    </p>
  </DocsShell>
</Base>
